{
  "dom_id": "step-q82851",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is the radius of convergence $R$ of the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{{5}^{2(n+1)}}=\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{5}^{6}}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{5}^{8}}+\u22ef\\,?\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{{5}^{2(n+1)}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{{5}^{2(n+2)}}\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{2(n+1)}}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}<25\\,\u21d2\\,|x|<\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=5.$",
